HBO's Chernobyl purports to show not  just the causes of the historic nuclear  accident but the bureaucracy that stood  in the way of rescue and recovery in the  aftermath with the real-life history  being shrouded in such secrecy it's a  story that can't help but leave some  questions unanswered a lot of numbers  get thrown around in Chernobyl  when Valeri Lagasse explains the worst  case scenario to the Soviet Central  Committee we get figures of potential  fatalities that are downright  catastrophic when the gossip gives the  numbers needed to contain the disaster  from the cost it demands to the years it  will take and the lives that will be  lost in the process  it sounds like horrific science-fiction  Lucia and the Ukraine impact means  completely uninhabitable for a minimum  of 100 years but one number audiences  don't get is a concrete death toll part  of the problem is the Soviet Union's  lack of records relating to the topic  but another trickier issue is the way  radiation exposure actually causes death  while many people lost their lives in  the disasters immediate aftermath there  were also untold numbers whose deaths  were more indirect caused by radiation  related illnesses that took years to  develop notes at the end of the series  put the death toll between 4,000 and 93  thousand but those numbers are far from  universally accepted the us-based  Union of Concerned Scientists estimates  between 4,000 and 27,000 lives lost  while Greenpeace believes it to be in  the range of 93 thousand to 200,000  Chernobyl showrunner Craig Mazin also  shared estimates he's read that put the  death toll closer to a million the  estimates are so varied that  unfortunately when it comes to the  question of how many people died from  the Chernobyl disaster the only answer  we can all agree on is too many one of  the most heartbreaking chapters in  Chernobyl comes in the 4th episode in  which we follow a trio of Soviet  Liquidators tasked with killing any  animals mostly domesticated pets left  behind in abandoned villages fleeing  civilians were not allowed to bring pets  with them for the fear the pets could  contaminate humans while the episode was  difficult for some viewers to watch  showrunner Craig Mazin has said that his  dramatized story is still a toned-down  version of what actually occurred  radioactive  so they have to go but it's not hard  they're mostly pets they're happy to see  you they run right up to you  bang but did the liquidators really  manage to wipe out all the pets who are  left behind in the evacuations not quite  according to the SPCA international and  the clean futures fund who frequently  visit the Chernobyl exclusion zone to  provide spaying neutering and  vaccination services over 800 dogs and  cats still live in the area subsisting  off of volunteer donations and their own  foraging skills to survive in 2019 SPCA  International announced that for the  first time 15 dogs with radiation levels  low enough to be considered safe were  taken from the exclusion zone and  adopted by homes in the United States  Chernobyl's fourth episode opens with an  elderly woman milking a cow while a  Soviet soldier demands that she evacuate  the scene is inspired by true stories of  people who return to their homes in the  exclusion zone in the years following  the meltdown  despite being legally barred from doing  so amazingly many of those people are  still there call them courageous or just  plain reckless Oh God and a routine code  live here on a permanent basis in 2017  the Irish Times visited the so called  some mostly for self settlers those who  illegally returned to Chernobyl to live  there after the disaster despite  warnings from scientists The Times said  around 200 people still made their homes  there some of whom were living well into  their 80s but don't take their presents  as evidence that the exclusion zone is  safe while residents may not be dropping  dead in the grisly manners of  Chernobyl's first responders cancer  diagnosis particularly thyroid cancer  and leukemia have spiked since the  accident despite these real dangers the  lives of the self settlers aren't nearly  as post-apocalyptic as you might think  and according to some who shared their  stories with the media things where they  live are just fine though the residents  aren't officially sanctioned by the  government their presence is tolerated  and they're given limited support  luchenko have been married 53 years they  say they don't need anything from anyone  the true noble series opens two years  after the disaster with Valeri Lagasse  recording his recollections on audio  tapes before hanging himself the gossip  makes like he's taking out the trash to  fool his KGB watchman and uses the  opportunity to drop the tapes  a nearby building a note at the end of  the series finale tells us that  Lagasse's records were circulated among  the soviet scientific community which  seems to confirm that this part of the  story is accurate however we never learn  hula gossip expects to retrieve the  tapes we don't know if he's been in  contact with a journalist or perhaps  with another scientist it's not clear  how his influential records actually get  out into the world speaking to the  cheatsheet about how he prepared for the  role of ligase Jared Harris said one of  his biggest challenges was that there  was little historical record left on the  gossip to study just like it's  threatened in the show the Soviet Union  did indeed do a good deal to erase him  from history Harris also said he never  listened to the gossips audio tapes and  he has good reason they don't exist  he explained there were no audio tapes  he left behind journals but that's not  as cinematic as audio tapes Chernobyl's  conclusion includes a 2006 quote from  Mikhail Gorbachev the Soviet Union's  final leader it reads the nuclear  meltdown at Chernobyl was perhaps the  true cause of the collapse of the Soviet  Union but the series doesn't explore  what exactly that statement means in  2013 mark Joseph Stern wrote an article  for Slate explaining what he called the  nuclear theory of the USSR's collapse  he explained that shortly before the  explosion at Chernobyl Gorbachev had  already introduced the policy of  glasnost or openness of ideas expression  in institutional transparency Stern  wrote that it was Gorbachev's way of  combating the widespread practice of  censoring information and keeping  secrets that the Chernobyl series does  such a good job of conveying the policy  of glasnost is widely credited with  fueling the USSR's eventual disillusion  in 1991 the aftermath of the Chernobyl  accident Stern argues is what wound up  fueling Blaz knows the most Stern wrote  that before Chernobyl most Soviet  citizens quote believed in the Soviet  system forgave its flaws and hoped for a  better future but the reactor explosion  and everything that came to light  afterwards put questions in the minds of  citizens crucial questions about what  was in their food and water whether they  should believe what the government was  telling them that new distrust for the  Soviet state according to the theory  made the USSR's demise inevitable the  horrible aftermath of Chernobyl as  depicted in HBO's miniseries  probably has plenty of viewers wondering  if what happened in 1986 Ukraine could  happen again in the United States or  elsewhere in 2016 Business Insider  talked to Chernobyl expert and author  Andrew leather Barrow the writer and  photographer took the opportunity to  explain why what happened at Chernobyl  simply couldn't happen in the US the key  he says is graphite as the series makes  clear in its final episode the power  plants graphite tipped control rods are  what caused the reactor explosion  accelerating the nuclear reaction  instead of helping to shut it down  according to leather Barrow using those  rods in the real-life reactor was quote  an incredibly foolish decision  thankfully American reactors use water  instead of graphite to serve that  function as leather Baro explained to  Business Insider there's absolutely no  way the accident at Chernobyl could have  happened at any American commercial  power reactor but before you breathe  easier this doesn't mean a meltdown  can't happen at a nuclear power plant in  the u.s. Americans learn to meltdown at  one of their plants was possible in 1979  s Three Mile Island incident just as the  Japanese learned in 2011 with the  Fukushima meltdown but the specific  event that triggered the Chernobyl  accident couldn't happen in the u.s.  meaning the chance of any Americans  having to stumble around shovel and  graphite off of rooftops in the years to  come is pretty much close to zero check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite shows are coming soon subscribe  to our YouTube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

